# Nexora AI: An AI-Driven Career Guidance and Placement Preparation Platform

S. SriDharani, S. Sharmila, S. Vidhya

Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hindustan Institute of Technology and Science, Chennai, India

Abstract—Career guidance and placement preparation are increasingly important in a rapidly changing job market where students and early-career professionals need personalized, data-informed support. Traditional systems often provide generic recommendations and are fragmented across multiple tools, which reduces efficiency and makes decision-making more difficult. This paper presents Nexora AI, a unified AI-driven career guidance and placement preparation platform designed to support career discovery, skill-gap analysis, roadmap generation, AI resume optimization, interview practice, and readiness tracking. The platform integrates Next.js, Clerk authentication, Prisma with PostgreSQL, Gemini-based generative AI, and Inngest for background workflows. The proposed system is designed to combine structured user data with AI-generated recommendations to improve personalization, reduce ambiguity in career decisions, and support students throughout their preparation journey. The implementation includes recently added placement-focused modules such as student profiling, diagnostic assessment, evidence-aware readiness scoring, adaptive practice, company preparation, mock interview evaluation, job tracking, and progress dashboards. The architecture is modular, scalable, and suitable for future extension with external learning and employment data sources.

Index Terms—Artificial Intelligence, Career Guidance, Generative AI, Resume Optimization, ATS Analysis, Interview Preparation, Skill Gap Analysis, Placement Readiness, Personalized Learning.

I. INTRODUCTION

Career planning is a critical step in students’ academic and professional development. In the modern digital era, students are exposed to a wide range of opportunities but often lack clear guidance on which career path matches their strengths, interests, and market demand. Traditional counselling mechanisms are often static, manual, and limited in scope, making it difficult to provide strong personalization for diverse user profiles. In addition, many learners need support across multiple stages of development, including skill assessment, roadmap generation, resume preparation, interview practice, and placement readiness evaluation.

The rapid growth of digital technologies has led to the emergence of online career platforms; however, these tools are frequently disconnected. Students typically toggle between separate services for skill learning, resume building, role exploration, and mock interviews. This fragmented experience reduces productivity and weakens the continuity of career planning. There is therefore a clear need for a unified system that supports the full career lifecycle from role discovery to job preparation.

This paper proposes Nexora AI, an AI-driven career guidance and placement preparation platform that integrates multiple career-support functions into a single ecosystem. The system collects structured user data such as education, skills, interests, target roles, and career goals. It then uses generative AI to produce personalized recommendations, including role suggestions, skill-gap analysis, learning plans, technical interview questions, and resume optimization suggestions. The platform further includes placement-oriented modules such as readiness evaluation, practice assessments, company and role preparation, mock interview support, and application tracking.

The main contribution of this work is the design and implementation of a unified system that connects career exploration with practical outcomes. Instead of suggesting isolated opportunities, the platform supports a continuous preparation workflow where the user moves from initial profile creation to assessment, skill improvement, interview preparation, and eventual placement support. This integrated design makes the platform relevant not only for career discovery but also for professional readiness and job preparation.

II. RELATED WORK

Recent research in educational technology and career recommendation systems has explored the use of rule-based systems, classification models, and recommendation techniques to match users to jobs. Several systems focus on identifying career paths using user profiles, academic history, skills, and preferences. While these systems can suggest relevant occupations, they often provide only limited personalization and rarely connect career matching with actionable learning and placement support.

Skill-gap analysis is another active area in educational research. These methods compare a learner’s current skill set with the skills required for a target job role. Although skill-gap detection is useful, many existing approaches treat it as a separate functionality rather than integrating it with roadmap generation, resume optimization, and interview preparation.

Similarly, applicant tracking systems (ATS) and resume analysis tools are widely used in recruitment pipelines. These tools score resumes based on keyword match, structure, and job relevance. However, many solutions focus mainly on resume scoring and do not provide end-to-end career planning and preparation support.

Generative AI and large language models have created new opportunities for personalized assistance in career guidance. Models can generate resumes, cover letters, roadmaps, interview questions, and learning recommendations based on user inputs. However, standalone AI assistants often lack persistent user profiles and structured workflow support. They also do not always integrate role-specific assessment, skill analysis, and application progress tracking into a single system.

Nexora AI addresses these limitations by combining AI-generated recommendations with a persistent user profile and a structured preparation workflow. This makes the platform more practical and relevant for real-world career decision support.

III. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND DESIGN

The Nexora AI platform is implemented as a modular full-stack web application. A typical workflow includes user authentication, profile creation, data storage, AI-powered reasoning, and result generation. The system architecture combines multiple layers to make the platform maintainable, scalable, and easy to extend.

A. Frontend Layer

The frontend is built using Next.js, which provides a modular and responsive interface for page-based application design. It supports onboarding, dashboard views, role discovery, interview practice, resume workflows, and placement-preparation modules. The user-facing interface is designed to be intuitive and accessible, enabling users to navigate the complete career-support journey.

B. Authentication and Authorization

Clerk is used for secure sign-in and sign-up processes. It manages authentication and protects routes that require a signed-in user. This ensures that user-specific data is only accessible to the appropriate account. The internal application profile is linked to the authenticated Clerk identity, enabling secure personalization across the system.

C. Data Management Layer

The platform stores structured user data in PostgreSQL using Prisma ORM. This includes personal profile information, skill sets, assessment results, practice history, career recommendations, generated roadmaps, interview transcripts, resume documents, and job tracking data. Structured persistence ensures consistency and allows modules to reuse information across the system.

D. AI and Generative Layer

The system integrates Google Gemini API to generate contextual outputs based on user profile data. Gemini is used for resume writing, ATS-oriented suggestions, career recommendations, roadmap generation, interview questions, and role-specific insights. Prompt-based generation is used to tailor outputs to each user’s education, goals, skills, and work experience.

E. Background Processing Layer

Inngest is used for asynchronous background tasks such as delayed AI processing, analysis jobs, or workflow orchestration. This improves responsiveness by allowing long-running operations to be processed without blocking the main user interaction flow.

F. Document and Resume Processing Layer

The platform includes document-processing features for resume upload and analysis. Uploaded files can be parsed, and AI-based or rule-based analysis can help identify weak sections, missing keywords, and alignment issues with a target job description.

IV. PROPOSED METHODOLOGY

The methodology follows a structured process beginning with profile creation and ending with personalized support and progress tracking.

A. User Profiling

The system begins by collecting user information such as education background, experience, skills, preferred domain, career goal, bio, and availability for learning. This profile acts as the foundation for all subsequent recommendations and personalization.

B. Career Recommendation

Once the profile is available, the platform compares the user's data with potential career roles. It evaluates factors such as educational fit, skill alignment, experience, work interests, and role demand. The output is a list of recommended career paths with reasoning and supporting details.

C. Skill Gap Analysis

The system compares current skills with the competencies required for the selected role. The difference between the two sets helps identify missing skills and highlights the learning areas that should be prioritized. This step is essential because it moves the system beyond generic suggestions and supports actionable personal development.

D. Roadmap and Course Recommendation

After the skill gaps are identified, the platform creates a structured roadmap with milestones, learning steps, and recommended resources. Course suggestions are generated based on the user’s target role, missing skills, and level of readiness. This improves clarity and allows users to study with a focused plan.

E. Resume and Job Material Support

The system supports resume creation, optimization, and ATS analysis. It can generate ATS-friendly resumes, scan existing resumes against job requirements, and suggest improvements for wording, formatting, and relevant keywords. It also supports cover-letter generation for job-specific applications.

F. Interview Preparation and Evaluation

The platform includes mock interview support based on role-specific and company-specific questions. It stores responses, generates follow-up prompts, and provides feedback on answer quality and relevance. This helps users improve communication, confidence, and technical understanding before actual interviews.

G. Placement Readiness and Evidence-Aware Scoring

A major enhancement in the developed implementation is the placement-preparation workspace. This module evaluates readiness using evidence such as assessment results, practice performance, skills, and project experience. The system provides readiness summaries and identifies areas needing attention. This approach is more realistic than a simple score because it distinguishes between measured evidence and missing or unknown inputs.

V. NEW FEATURES AND MODULES ADDED TO THE SYSTEM

The current implementation extends Nexora AI beyond a conventional career assistant by including a broader set of placement-preparation modules. These are described below.

1. Student Profile and Authentication Module: Handles account creation, user onboarding, and personal profile management.
2. Career and Interest Assessment Module: Evaluates aptitude, reasoning, technical knowledge, communication, and role interests.
3. Placement Readiness and Skill Analysis Module: Assesses readiness using evidence-based performance and skill coverage.
4. Personalized Preparation Engine: Generates weekly plans and tasks aligned with skill gaps and readiness goals.
5. Assessment and Practice Center: Provides aptitude, DSA, SQL, reasoning, and technical practice workflows.
6. Progressive Level System: Tracks learning progression and skill maturity across multiple assessment attempts.
7. Intelligent Mistake Analysis: Identifies incorrect patterns and suggests targeted remediation strategies.
8. Company and Role Preparation Module: Supports job-role and company-specific preparation planning.
9. AI Roadmap Generator: Produces structured learning milestones and action plans for a chosen role.
10. Course and Learning Recommendation Module: Suggests learning paths based on missing skills and goals.
11. Resume Intelligence Module: Combines resume building, ATS scanning, optimization, and cover-letter generation.
12. AI Mock Interview Agent: Provides interview simulations with role-based questions and follow-up queries.
13. AI Interview Evaluation Module: Assesses interview responses and provides structured feedback.
14. Interview Integrity and Anti-Malpractice Module: Logs browser events and flagged behaviors during assessments.
15. Job Recommendation and Matching Module: Compares user profile and resume with job descriptions.
16. Job Application Tracker: Helps users manage application status from applied to interview, offer, or rejection.
17. Placement Journey Tracker: Consolidates readiness, practice, interviews, and job application progress.
18. Student Progress Dashboard: Provides a central overview of assessments, skill readiness, and preparation tasks.

These features collectively transform the project from a general career guidance system into a broader student career and placement support ecosystem.

VI. IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS

The implementation uses a modern web stack and follows a consistent request-processing pattern. User actions are handled through frontend components that trigger server actions or API endpoints. The server authenticates the user, validates input, loads or updates the database, and then invokes AI logic when necessary. This architecture encourages maintainability and ensures that user data remains connected to the relevant recommendation and preparation modules.

The first important step is the onboarding and profile creation phase. The user provides essential information such as career interests, education, professional experience, and skills. These values are stored in a persistent profile and become the main input to all later modules. Once this profile is created, the user can access the career dashboard, skill analysis, interviews, resume tools, and placement-preparation workspace.

After profile creation, the user can run assessments and access recommendation modules. These functions rely on stored profile data and AI-based reasoning to provide insight. Results such as recommendations, roadmap tasks, and resume suggestions are saved so that they can be reused in subsequent sessions. This persistent approach improves personalization and continuity.

VII. ADVANTAGES AND RESEARCH SIGNIFICANCE

Nexora AI is significant because it integrates several functionalities that are often fragmented across separate platforms. The platform provides a single environment for career discovery, learning planning, resume enhancement, interview preparation, and placement support. This unified design improves usability and allows students to manage their preparation from one interface.

The platform also demonstrates how modern full-stack technologies and generative AI can be used together for practical educational and career support. Instead of making isolated recommendations, it constructs a continuous decision-support workflow. This makes it valuable for both research and real deployment contexts.

The research relevance of the system lies in its combination of structured user data, AI-generated reasoning, and modular application architecture. It offers a scalable model for future systems that support career guidance, assessment, and employability preparation.

VIII. LIMITATIONS

Although the platform is conceptually well designed, it has several practical limitations that should be acknowledged. First, the quality of recommendations depends on the quality and completeness of user input. Second, AI-generated responses should be treated as decision-support content rather than guaranteed results. Third, the system relies on external services such as Gemini API and browser-based features such as speech recognition, which depend on configuration and environment support. Finally, role-fit and readiness indicators are application-generated estimates based on available evidence and should not be interpreted as validated hiring predictions or employment guarantees.

IX. CONCLUSION

This paper presents Nexora AI, an AI-driven platform designed to support personalized career guidance and placement preparation in a unified workflow. The system combines user profiling, skill-gap analysis, roadmap generation, resume optimization, interview support, and readiness evaluation into a single architecture. The newly added modules extend the system beyond generic career assistance and make it more suitable for practical student preparation and placement support.

The platform demonstrates the practical value of combining modern web development, structured database systems, and generative AI in a cohesive environment. By integrating career guidance and placement preparation into one system, Nexora AI presents a strong foundation for future research and development in intelligent career support systems.

X. FUTURE WORK

Future work can focus on improving the quality and reliability of recommendations, integrating verified external job and learning data, expanding assessment reliability, and conducting user studies to evaluate the effectiveness of the system in real-world decision-making. Additional work can also be directed toward stronger privacy safeguards, more transparent scoring, and improved interoperability with professional and learning platforms.

